course: biology
lesson: 151
title: Myth check: do ostriches bury their heads in the sand?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In cartoons a scared ostrich pushes its head into the sand and thinks it is hidden, with its fluffy bottom still sticking up in the air.
explain: Real ostriches never do this: a bird with its head under sand could not breathe, and it would be easy prey for a lion.
explain: The myth probably grew from what people saw from far away: an ostrich bending down to turn its eggs in a pit in the ground, or lying flat with its neck along the soil, looks headless.
explain: A real ostrich in danger does something much cleverer: it runs at about 70 km per hour, kicks with a clawed foot, or lies flat so its brown-grey feathers blend with the ground.
fact: Ostriches do not bury their heads in sand; they could not breathe, and it would not make them safer.
fact: Ostriches lay their eggs in a shallow pit scraped in the ground and several times a day put their heads in to turn the eggs with their beaks, so the eggs warm evenly and the chick does not stick to the shell.
fact: An ostrich that senses danger and cannot run may lie flat with its neck on the ground, so it is hard to spot from far away.
fact: Ostriches can run at about 70 km per hour, faster than any other bird on land, with strides of about 4 to 5 metres.
fact: An ostrich kicks forwards and down, and a kick from its clawed foot can badly injure or even kill a lion or a person.
fact: Ostriches have only two toes on each foot, fewer than any other bird.
fact: An ostrich egg is the largest egg of any living bird, about 15 centimetres long and as heavy as about 20 to 24 hen's eggs; its shell is so strong that a grown-up can stand on it.
fact: An ostrich's eye is about 5 centimetres across, the biggest of any land animal and bigger than its own brain.
fact: Ostriches swallow pebbles, which collect in the gizzard and grind tough food, because birds have no teeth.
fact: «Burying your head in the sand» means pretending a problem is not there; the saying comes from the myth, not from real ostriches.
fact: Ostrich chicks from several nests may be gathered into one big group looked after by a few adults, and chicks can run fast within days of hatching.
fact: Ostriches cannot fly, but their wings help them balance, steer and turn sharply when running, shade their chicks and cool them on hot days.
fact: The Roman writer Pliny the Elder wrote almost 2,000 years ago that ostriches hide their heads in bushes, and the story grew from there.
exercise: Do real ostriches bury their heads in the sand to hide? | No, they could not breathe and it would not keep them safe | Yes, whenever a lion comes near, so the lion thinks it has gone | Yes, but only when they are asleep | Yes, but only the chicks do it
exercise: Why does an ostrich put its head down into the pit in the ground? | To turn its eggs with its beak | To hide from lions | To drink water from under the sand | To listen for enemies underground
exercise: Why does a parent ostrich turn its eggs? | So they warm evenly and the chick does not stick to the shell | So the eggs roll away from enemies | So the chick inside gets dizzy, turns round and round and hatches sooner | So the shell gets thinner
exercise: What does an ostrich do if it senses danger but cannot run? | It lies flat with its neck stretched along the ground | It digs a hole and climbs into it | It flies up into a tree | It pushes its whole head under the sand and keeps very still until it is safe
exercise: About how fast can an ostrich run? | About 70 km per hour | About 7 km per hour | About 25 km per hour | About 300 km per hour
exercise: What is an ostrich's real way to escape danger? | Running away very fast | Hiding its head | Flying away | Playing dead for hours
exercise: Which way does an ostrich kick? | Forwards and down | Backwards like a horse | Sideways only | Straight up into the air
exercise: How many toes does an ostrich have on each foot? | Two | Three | Four | Five
exercise: About how many hen's eggs weigh as much as one ostrich egg? | About 20 to 24 | About 2 | About 5 | About 200
exercise: Which is bigger than an ostrich's brain? | One of its eyes | Its egg tooth | One of its toes | Nothing, its brain is huge
exercise: Why does an ostrich swallow pebbles? | They grind tough food in its gizzard because birds have no teeth | It likes the taste of stones | They make it heavy so the strong desert wind cannot blow it over when it runs | It is cleaning the sand for its eggs
exercise: What does «burying your head in the sand» mean? | Pretending a problem is not there | Being very good at hiding | Being brave in danger | Looking for something you lost
exercise: Why are many ostrich chicks gathered into one big group? | A few adults can protect many chicks at once | The chicks are lost and cannot find home | The adults want to count them | Ostriches lay all their eggs in one nest, so every chick there is a brother or sister
exercise: What do an ostrich's wings do if it cannot fly? | Help it balance, steer and turn when running | Nothing at all, they are useless and just get in the way when it runs | Let it glide off cliffs | Dig holes in the sand
exercise: Who wrote an early version of the ostrich myth almost 2,000 years ago? | Pliny the Elder, a Roman writer | A modern cartoon studio | Charles Darwin | A zoo keeper in London
test: In a cartoon an ostrich hides its head in the sand and the lion walks past. What is true? | A real ostrich would run, kick or lie flat; with its head in the sand it could not even breathe | That is exactly what real ostriches do | It works because lions cannot see bottoms | Only cartoon lions are fooled, but real lions are fooled by it too, which is why ostriches still do it
test: «my uncle saw an ostrich with its head in the ground at the safari park, so the myth's real innit» What is right? | It was probably turning its eggs in the nest pit, which looks like that from far away | Yes, so the myth is true | It was hiding from your uncle | It was eating the sand for lunch, because ostriches need sand to make their eggshells
test: Three ostriches stand in a row. How many toes do they have altogether? | 12 | 18 | 6 | 30
test: Two ostriches walk past. How many legs and wings do they have altogether? | 4 legs and 4 wings | 2 legs and 2 wings | 8 legs and 4 wings | 4 legs and no wings
test: «mate says ostriches are thick, they hide their heads and leave their bum out lol» What is the best answer? | They are not foolish: they turn their eggs carefully, guard their chicks and run very fast | He's right, they're the dumbest birds | Yes, but only the males do it | Yes, because their brain is smaller than their eyes, so they cannot think properly at all
test: What would happen to an ostrich that really kept its head under sand for a long time? | It would run out of air | It would hear lions better | It would cool down nicely | Nothing, it can breathe sand
test: A lion creeps towards a resting ostrich on the open plain. What will the ostrich most likely do? | Spot it with its huge eyes and sprint away | Bury its head and wait | Fly straight up into the nearest tall tree and wait there | Sit on the lion
test: «stand on an ostrich egg? it'll smash, obviously» What is right? | Its shell is so strong a grown-up can stand on it | Yes, it's as thin as a hen's egg | Yes, they are so thin that they break if you even look at them | It only holds if it is cooked
test: Your teacher says: «Stop burying your head in the sand about the homework!» What does she mean? | You are ignoring a problem you need to face | You are very good at hiding things | You should do your homework outside | You are playing too much in the sandpit and should come in now
test: Why might a lying-down ostrich be safer than a standing one? | A big bird flat on the ground is much harder to spot from far away | Lions never eat birds that are lying down, because they think they are dead | It can kick better lying down | It is closer to its buried head
test: «ostrich wings are just for show, right» What is right? | They help it balance and steer when running, shade chicks and cool it on hot days | Yes, they do nothing at all | They let it fly short distances over rivers and up onto the tops of rocks | They are used to dig the nest
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the alleged practice of ostriches burying their heads in sand. | The practice does not occur; the belief arose from observation of the bird lowering its head to the nest | The practice occurs habitually when a predator approaches, the bird believing itself thereby concealed from view | The practice occurs exclusively among juvenile birds | The practice is confined to periods of sleep
exam: Identify the principal means by which the ostrich evades a predator. | Flight on foot at a velocity of approximately seventy kilometres per hour | Concealment of the head beneath the surface of the ground until the predator has withdrawn | Ascent into the branches of trees | Prolonged feigning of death
exam: Ascertain the aggregate number of toes possessed by two adult ostriches, given that each foot bears the number characteristic of the species. | Eight | Twelve | Sixteen | Twenty
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of the stones swallowed by the ostrich? | They are retained in the gizzard and grind food, the bird possessing no teeth | They are swallowed accidentally and serve no purpose | They are regurgitated by the bird and arranged in a circle in order to construct the nest | They serve as ballast against strong winds
